2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - **M7 ② particle_flow_view**: Pure-LVGL C differentiation page (second round-screen page alongside pet). 18 particles with integer fixed-point Brownian motion + state-dependent drift, round-bound bounce, pulsing core. Same 6-state agent lifecycle palette as pet (green/cyan/blue/orange/yellow/red). Integer-only physics (position int32 px, velocity q8 where 256=1px/tick, dist_sq no sqrt, per-particle LCG noise, no float/math.h). lv_timer @33ms on LVGL thread. Module pattern mirrors pet_status_view (init/show/hide/is_visible/set_state, lv_async_call thread safety).
   - **THE KEY FINDING — qemu goldfish post-boot lv_screen_load flush-binding limitation**: A screen loaded via lv_screen_load AT BOOT binds to the fb flush path (content writes /dev/fb0). A screen loaded via lv_screen_load AFTER BOOT (post-boot, e.g. via set_page 1 or first-message chat show) does NOT bind — its content (even bg) never writes /dev/fb0, fb0 stays boot white 0xf7be. Proven via A/B: same code, same timer, same flush callback — boot-screen particle flushes (57 values, navy+green+blue), post-boot set_page-1 particle stays white. Pet (boot home screen) flushes; particle (set_page 1 secondary) doesn't. Real CP /dev/lcd0 flush is screen-agnostic (putarea doesn't distinguish boot/secondary), so set_page 1 works on real HW.
   - **A/B verification method (general, reusable)**: To verify rendering of a post-boot secondary screen in qemu fb0, temporarily add at the end of its init: `lv_screen_load(target_screen); lv_obj_invalidate(target_screen); lv_refr_now(lv_display_get_default()); <set visible>=true;` — this makes it the boot screen, bypassing the post-boot flush-binding limitation. Verify via fb0 dump, then REVERT the diagnostic. Applied to particle (57 values) and chat screen (104 values). Always revert before final commit.
   - **Conditional set_pos optimization**: Particle velocity is q8; most ticks a particle moves <1px, so unconditional lv_obj_set_pos invalidated 18 regions every 33ms even when nothing visibly moved. Track each particle's last rendered pixel top-left (last_rx/last_ry); only call lv_obj_set_pos when the integer position actually changes. This collapses invalidations to real pixel crossings. (Did NOT fix the qemu secondary-screen issue alone, but is a good optimization kept.)
